<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sao Kya Seng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1924–1962</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sao Kya Seng was the last saopha of Hsipaw, a Shan state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, educated as a mining engineer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where he met his Austrian wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inge-sargent">Inge Sargent</a>. He introduced land reforms and sat in the Burmese parliament. He was arrested during General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a>'s military coup of March 1962 and disappeared; his wife later told his story in the memoir Twilight over Burma.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sao-kya-seng.jpg" alt="Sao Kya Seng" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sao_Kya_Seng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
